#pragma once
#include "/UltraLargeScale/GalaxyDensityCore.ush"
// --- RAYMARCH: ANALYTIC SPHERE-BOUNDED DENSITY ACCUMULATION ---
//
// The proxy mesh decides which pixels run; the unit sphere decides where to march.
//
// THE RAY IS BUILT FROM THE CAMERA, NOT FROM THE SHADED SURFACE.
//
// This used to take the proxy's shaded position and intersect from there, which dragged
// in three things that have all now gone: a box-intersect node in the material graph to
// produce it, a signed-t special case because an INVERTED-NORMAL proxy shades the FAR
// face so the chord lay at negative t, and a dCam correction to recover camera distance
// from a surface-relative parameter. The march never needed the surface -- it needed a
// ray, and the camera position plus the view direction already are one.
//
// InViewVec is the LOCAL camera vector, SURFACE TOWARD CAMERA -- unchanged, still
// CameraVector through TransformVector [World -> Local]. Direction only; normalized or
// not makes no difference.
//
// InCamPosN is the camera in NORMALIZED space, the box spanning -1 to 1. NO REMAP IS
// APPLIED TO IT HERE. It is the ray ORIGIN rather than only a distance reference, so a
// wrong value does not merely bias step sizes -- it moves the ray. That is a promotion
// in consequence: the old failure was a plausible image at the wrong cost, the new one
// is visibly wrong geometry, which is the better way round.
//
// RETURNS (emission, steps taken, 0, transmittance).
//
// The step count rides in G because cost has no other observer: a march that truncates
// looks identical to one that finished, except that the far half of the volume is not
// there. RAW COUNT, not normalized -- the material divides by whatever range it wants,
// and normalizing here would be one more number to keep in agreement with the loop bound.
//
// ==========================================================================
// THE STEPPING RULE IS THE UNIVERSE LAYER'S. THE INTERSECTION IS STILL THE GALAXY'S.
// ==========================================================================
// Everything below -- b, c, disc, tEnter, tExit, the behind-camera early out, the
// camera-inside case falling out of max(tEnter, 0) -- is unchanged and was never in
// question. Only the STEP SIZING moved:
//
//     baseStep = span / StepBudget
//     h        = baseStep * (1 + StepGrowth * t01)
//
// where t01 is progress along the marched span, not distance from the camera.
//
// WHAT WAS GIVEN UP, stated plainly, because it was a real property: sizing each step as
// a fraction of camera distance keeps every step the same size ON SCREEN, which is
// genuinely better for a proxy viewed from outside, and the old comments were right that
// the two bounds rather than the ratio dominated the cost.
//
// WHAT WAS BOUGHT: a cost that can be SET rather than DISCOVERED. Under the old rule the
// step count depended on where the camera was, collapsing toward chord/MinStep on close
// approach -- at the shipped defaults the floor took over just outside the volume, so
// every close pass ran the ceiling budget. With four marchers able to be on screen at
// once, per-layer cost has to be a number chosen up front. This rule makes the count
// StepBudget * ln(1+g)/g whatever the viewer is doing.
//
// The two rules still agree about the far field. They disagree near the entry face,
// where the camera-relative step went toward zero and this one does not.
//
// STEPBUDGET IS A BUDGET, NOT A COUNT. Growth means the steps overshoot the span before
// the budget is spent, so the loop finishes early: the actual count is
// StepBudget * ln(1 + growth) / growth -- the budget itself at growth 0, 0.69 of it at
// growth 1, 0.55 at growth 2. See FGalaxyMaterialParams::GetEffectiveStepCount, which is
// the same expression on the C++ side.
//
// THE FLOOR IS THE FIELD'S, NOT AN AUTHORED PIN. P.MinFeatureStep is half the narrowest
// thing the parameters can produce -- see GALAXY_MIN_STEP_FRACTION in the core. The
// authored MinStep it replaced was an absolute distance derived from a NearSteps budget,
// so it knew the chord but not the field, and could not be right for a thin disc and a
// thick one at once. A floor can only ever LENGTHEN a step, so this is a pure cost
// saving: it binds when the budget is set fine enough to resolve detail the field does
// not contain, which is exactly when it should.
//
// NO MINSAMPLES, and nothing replaces it. It capped the step at span/MinSamples so a
// distant galaxy could not resolve to a handful of samples; the first step is now
// span/StepBudget by construction and growth only ever lengthens from there, so coverage
// of the chord is structural rather than clamped.
//
// ARGUMENT ORDER CHANGED. The Custom node calls this positionally and a Custom node fails
// at SHADER compile rather than at build, so the node's pins must be rebuilt: StepRatio,
// MinStep and MinSamples are gone, StepBudget and StepGrowth are new, and MaxSteps is now
// derived in the node body as int(StepBudget) + 8 rather than pushed.
// FOUR TEXTURE PAIRS, held and never read -- this file marches, the core fetches. See the
// table at the top of GalaxyDensityCore.ush for which asset serves which fetch.
float4 GalaxyRayMarch(GalaxyDensityParams P,
                      Texture3DSigned InWarpTexGas, SamplerState InWarpSamplerGas,
                      Texture3DSigned InWarpTexHalo, SamplerState InWarpSamplerHalo,
                      Texture3D InNoiseTexGas, SamplerState InNoiseSamplerGas,
                      Texture3D InNoiseTexHalo, SamplerState InNoiseSamplerHalo,
                      float3 InViewVec, float3 InCamPosN,
                      float InStepBudget, float InStepGrowth, int InMaxSteps,
                      float InDensityScale, float InJitter, float InNoisePower)
{
    // THE CAMERA IS THE ORIGIN, so t IS the distance from the camera. Nothing downstream
    // reads that any more -- the step no longer does -- but the parameterisation is still
    // what makes the near clamp below a clamp rather than a reconstruction.
    float3 o = InCamPosN;
    float3 dir = normalize(-InViewVec);

    float b = dot(o, dir);
    float c = dot(o, o) - 1.0;
    float disc = b * b - c;
    if (disc <= 0.0)
    {
        return float4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
    }

    float sq = sqrt(disc);

    float tEnter = -b - sq;
    float tExit = -b + sq;

    // BEHIND THE CAMERA. With the ray anchored at the camera this is a real case rather
    // than an impossible one: the proxy is drawn with inverted normals, so its far face
    // can be rasterized while the volume itself is behind the viewer. The old
    // surface-anchored form could not express this and had no test for it.
    if (tExit <= 0.0)
    {
        return float4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
    }

    // CAMERA INSIDE THE VOLUME falls out of the same clamp rather than needing its own
    // branch: tEnter is negative there, so starting at zero starts at the camera. That
    // replaces the guarded dCam trim, which existed only to reconstruct this from a
    // surface-anchored parameterisation and could fail open to the full chord.
    //
    // IT MATTERS MORE UNDER THIS RULE THAN THE OLD ONE. A camera-relative step collapsed
    // to nothing over the stretch behind the viewer, so marching it was merely wasteful;
    // a span-relative step sizes that stretch as if it were part of the visible one, so
    // failing to trim it would spend budget on geometry nobody can see AND coarsen
    // everything that is visible.
    float tStart = max(tEnter, 0.0);
    if (tExit <= tStart)
    {
        return float4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
    }

    // THE SPAN IS WHAT SIZES THE STEP. The whole progression falls out of these two
    // lines: the distance the ray actually has to cover, divided by the budget. Note it
    // is the TRIMMED span, so a camera inside the volume budgets across what remains
    // ahead of it rather than across the full chord.
    float span = max(tExit - tStart, 1e-6);
    float baseStep = span / max(InStepBudget, 1.0);

    // See GALAXY_MIN_STEP_FRACTION. The 1e-5 is a loop-safety floor and nothing else:
    // MinFeatureStep is derived from clamped inputs and cannot reach zero, but a params
    // struct assembled by hand could.
    float minStep = max(P.MinFeatureStep, 1e-5);

    float growth = max(InStepGrowth, 0.0);
    float invSpan = 1.0 / span;

    // Jitter shortens the FIRST step only, offsetting each pixel's lattice independently
    // and breaking up the concentric banding a fixed lattice produces. It costs no energy:
    // the loop clamps the last step to the exit, so the total path is unchanged.
    float firstScale = clamp(InJitter, 0.05, 1.0);

    bool bShape = (InNoisePower != 1.0);

    float transmittance = 1.0;
    float emission = 0.0;
    float t = tStart;
    float stepsTaken = 0.0;

    // InMaxSteps is a SAFETY BOUND, not the step count. StepBudget and the growth rate
    // set the count between them; this only stops a pathological combination from
    // hanging the shader, and should sit just above StepBudget rather than well above it
    // -- a bound far from the budget can never be reached, and a bound below it truncates
    // silently.
    [loop]
    for (int i = 0; i < InMaxSteps; i++)
    {
        float remaining = tExit - t;
        if (remaining <= 1e-6)
        {
            break;
        }

        // THE STEP IS SIZED BEFORE THE SAMPLE IS TAKEN, because the sample sits at the
        // MIDPOINT and the midpoint is not known until the length is.
        //
        // GROWS WITH PROGRESS ALONG THE SPAN, not with distance from the camera. t01 is
        // saturated rather than trusted: tStart is clamped forward to the camera above,
        // and any arrangement that put it past tExit would otherwise drive the step
        // negative and hang the march.
        float t01 = saturate((t - tStart) * invSpan);
        float stepLen = max(baseStep * (1.0 + growth * t01), minStep);
        if (i == 0)
        {
            stepLen *= firstScale;
        }
        stepLen = min(stepLen, remaining);

        stepsTaken += 1.0;

        // Sampled at the step MIDPOINT. With uniform steps the choice only shifts the
        // result half a step; with steps that grow along the ray, sampling at the near
        // edge weights every interval by its own leading face and biases the whole
        // integral toward the camera -- systematically, and more so the faster the steps
        // grow, which is faster now than it was.
        //
        // DO NOT ADD A BOUNDS TEST HERE. The sphere chord already bounds the march, and
        // the field is zero outside the unit sphere anyway. A redundant test on the first
        // sample fails wherever rounding pushes tStart a hair outside, and because it is
        // view-direction dependent it shows as angle-dependent dropout at silhouettes.
        float density = GalaxySample(P,
            InWarpTexGas, InWarpSamplerGas,
            InWarpTexHalo, InWarpSamplerHalo,
            InNoiseTexGas, InNoiseSamplerGas,
            InNoiseTexHalo, InNoiseSamplerHalo,
            o + dir * (t + stepLen * 0.5));
        if (bShape)
        {
            density = P.PowSafe(density, InNoisePower);
        }

        // stepLen is already in the units LayerDensity was normalised against, so the
        // accumulated depth through a layer comes out as its authored value directly.
        float alpha = 1.0 - exp(-density * InDensityScale * stepLen);

        // Flat white emission: structure only.
        emission += alpha * transmittance;
        transmittance *= 1.0 - alpha;

        if (transmittance < 0.001)
        {
            break;
        }

        t += stepLen;
    }

    return float4(emission, stepsTaken, 0.0, transmittance);
}
